  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In March 2022, the Nigeria Employers&#x2019; Consultative Association (NECA) produced a report on the &#x201C;Next Normal: the changing workplace in Nigeria&#x201D;.  Based on a survey of 201 enterprises and 28 Key Informant Interviews, the report revealed how the Covid-19 pandemic affected work and the workplace in formal businesses in Nigeria. NECA conducted advocacy activities to share the findings and promote its recommendations, first through the launch of the report on 5 April 2022, attended by representatives of the Federal Ministry of Labour and Employment (FMLE), government institutions, Presidents of workers&#x2019; organizations, tripartite bodies and companies. NECA also developed and implemented a follow-up advocacy and action agenda for continuous engagement with the government and workers &#x2019;organizations on key issues relevant to Nigeria&#x2019;s current context. The issues include remote and hybrid work, employers&#x2019; duty of care, performance monitoring, performance-based pay, workplace relations, revisions to OSH policies regarding remote work, the agility of skills training systems to meet ever-changing skills demands, rapid quality assurance and certification of short courses and micro-credentials for on-demand skills training and policies that improve digital access and inclusion.
NECA pursued its engagement at a tripartite level through a presentation and discussion of the report during the 2023 session of the National Labour Advisory Commission (NLAC), the highest tripartite body in Nigeria on labour matters, held on 13 and 14 July 2023. At that session, NECA convinced the tripartite partners of the need to take the discussion further and have a common understanding and agreement on issues such as teleworking, particularly relevant considering the removal of government subsidy on fuel. Following this session NECA developed a report and policy brief on teleworking and business environment to form the basis of a policy paper to be shared with FMLE and the workers&#x2019; organizations. The ILO provided support to NECA in producing the &#x201C;Next Normal: the changing workplace in Nigeria&#x201D; report, which contributes to a global study commissioned in partnership with employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs) across Africa. The support entailed providing the design and methodology of the survey to allow comparisons at the regional level. The ILO reviewed the questionnaire, uploaded it on the Qualtrics online platform and shared the raw data from the survey as well as the reports produced by the platform. The ILO also reviewed the Key Informant Interviews and provided input. The ILO also reviewed the draft report and shared comments until the finalization of a report meeting its standards. The ILO supported NECA in the printing of 100 copies of the reports that have been disseminated since April 2022.
Based on the findings of the report and its recommendations, NECA was able to engage with the government and workers &#x2018;organizations on key issues to be considered beyond the pandemic.  The ILO advocated to get the presentation of the key findings and recommendations report in the agenda of the NLAC. 
The ILO also provided assistance to the development of NECA&#x2019;s report and policy brief on teleworking and reviewed the document until finalization.
In order to strengthen NECA capacity for evidence-based advocacy, NECA staff was trained on the Qualtrics survey tool and attended trainings at ITC ILO on : Designing and implementing working time arrangements and telework (22/03/2023-25/04/2022), &#x201C;-Online International training on evidence-based policy making for decent work (22/05/2023-31/05/2023), Data analytics and knowledge management for development (23/10/2023 &#x2013; 15/12/2023).</narrative>
   </description>
